{
  "abstract": "Can spatial constraints suppress the leading geometric fluctuations\nwithout sacrificing the counting entropy relevant to no-three-in-line sets?\nWe construct exact degree-one/two reference ensembles with prescribed\nspatial block counts at every fixed rational density $1<c<2$. Fine\nstratification retains entropy $cn\\log n+O(n)$ and reduces the incidence\nprojection to $o(n)$. The remaining second factorial cumulant of the triple\ncount has an explicit positive linear limit. Resumming triples into one\nindicator per offending line changes that limit to an explicit negative\nintegral. The proof uses a discrete incidence-energy estimate, uniform\npattern bounds, permutation Poincare inequalities and a factorially summable\nmixed covariance expansion. A weighted extension realizes the previously\ncertified density profile at $1.86637$ as an exact correlated reference law.\nWe also delimit these conclusions. Protected high-energy configurations\nforce the full-law factorial-cumulant radii to collapse, while a\nstretched-exponentially small energy cutoff preserves entropy, logarithmic\npattern estimates and the entire negative-real partition function. A\npositive rational profile has a positive formal entropy-minus-mean exponent\nbut an empty void event because it violates a diagonal capacity. Exact\narithmetic and independently checked finite configurations support the\nspecified formulas, without asserting effective asymptotic rates. Finally,\ncooling only to a linear repair budget reduces a sufficient construction\ntarget to an $O(\\log\\log n)$ temperature horizon. Its required thermal\nvariance lower bound remains unproved; no unbounded no-three family above\n$1.8n$ is constructed.",
  "author": "lattice_lift_research",
